{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E8py6WqRb6AC2t9saGZ9Zd","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Review Form Analysis\n\nBased on the provided content, I must report that **the complete review form criteria, scoring scale, and specific evaluation dimensions are not included** in this webpage excerpt.\n\n## What IS Available:\n\n**Review Criteria (General):**\nThe page states that submissions will be evaluated on \"the significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\" and their \"potential for scientific and technological impact, as well as their relevance to robotic learning.\"\n\n**Desk Reject Criteria:**\nPapers may be rejected for:\n- Formatting violations\n- Anonymity breaches\n- Scope issues (must demonstrate learning relevance to physical robots through either intent or outcome)\n\n## What IS Missing:\n\n- No numerical rating scale (1-10, etc.) is provided\n- No detailed evaluation rubric or scoring definitions\n- No specific review form fields are listed\n- No guidance on how to differentiate between rating levels\n\n**Recommendation:** You'll need to access the full CoRL 2024 review submission system or contact the organizing committee directly to obtain the complete review form template and scoring guidelines, as these details aren't published on this instruction page."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"f0386a10-df8a-4a3a-ae00-6125ab66aae2","timestamp":"2026-05-15T22:49:31.165Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":133849,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Review Form Analysis\n\nBased on the provided content, I must report that **the complete review form criteria, scoring scale, and specific evaluation dimensions are not included** in this webpage excerpt.\n\n## What IS Available:\n\n**Review Criteria (General):**\nThe page states that submissions will be evaluated on \"the significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\" and their \"potential for scientific and technological impact, as well as their relevance to robotic learning.\"\n\n**Desk Reject Criteria:**\nPapers may be rejected for:\n- Formatting violations\n- Anonymity breaches\n- Scope issues (must demonstrate learning relevance to physical robots through either intent or outcome)\n\n## What IS Missing:\n\n- No numerical rating scale (1-10, etc.) is provided\n- No detailed evaluation rubric or scoring definitions\n- No specific review form fields are listed\n- No guidance on how to differentiate between rating levels\n\n**Recommendation:** You'll need to access the full CoRL 2024 review submission system or contact the organizing committee directly to obtain the complete review form template and scoring guidelines, as these details aren't published on this instruction page.","durationMs":4030,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P2amnMJDT8Gpe2V5JZbBXC","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines - Scoring Information\n\nBased on the provided content, I must report that **the document does not include a numerical rating scale, specific score level definitions, or explicit accept/reject thresholds**.\n\n## What IS Provided:\n\nThe guidelines specify six evaluation dimensions:\n\n1. **Originality** - novelty of problems/methods and differentiation from prior work\n2. **Quality** - technical soundness and support for claims\n3. **Clarity** - writing quality and organization\n4. **Significance** - importance and potential impact\n5. **Relevance** - alignment with robot learning focus and CoRL audience interest\n6. **Limitations** - appreciation for honestly reported limitations; critical assessment of unreported shortcomings\n\n## Key Instruction:\n\nThe guidelines emphasize that \"CoRL papers are required to include a limitations section\" and stress treating disclosed limitations favorably while \"extensively\" noting undisclosed flaws.\n\n## Notable Absence:\n\nThe document provides examples of \"reviewer mistakes to avoid\" but deliberately avoids rigid scoring rules. For instance, it notes that papers need not \"beat SOTA\" for acceptance nor are \"dataset publication\" or \"real robot demos\" strict requirements.\n\nThe guidelines prioritize **constructive, substantiated feedback** over mechanistic scoring, directing reviewers to ICML and NeurIPS resources for additional guidance on review quality."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"b5a8463c-9658-4299-bdb1-29c0b5274bb0","timestamp":"2026-05-15T22:49:33.433Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Reviewer Guidelines - Scoring Information\n\nBased on the provided content, I must report that **the document does not include a numerical rating scale, specific score level definitions, or explicit accept/reject thresholds**.\n\n## What IS Provided:\n\nThe guidelines specify six evaluation dimensions:\n\n1. **Originality** - novelty of problems/methods and differentiation from prior work\n2. **Quality** - technical soundness and support for claims\n3. **Clarity** - writing quality and organization\n4. **Significance** - importance and potential impact\n5. **Relevance** - alignment with robot learning focus and CoRL audience interest\n6. **Limitations** - appreciation for honestly reported limitations; critical assessment of unreported shortcomings\n\n## Key Instruction:\n\nThe guidelines emphasize that \"CoRL papers are required to include a limitations section\" and stress treating disclosed limitations favorably while \"extensively\" noting undisclosed flaws.\n\n## Notable Absence:\n\nThe document provides examples of \"reviewer mistakes to avoid\" but deliberately avoids rigid scoring rules. For instance, it notes that papers need not \"beat SOTA\" for acceptance nor are \"dataset publication\" or \"real robot demos\" strict requirements.\n\nThe guidelines prioritize **constructive, substantiated feedback** over mechanistic scoring, directing reviewers to ICML and NeurIPS resources for additional guidance on review quality.","durationMs":4920,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
