{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vjq5e9uCBjV82eFmJVQnqD","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2021 Review Form Information\n\nBased on the provided webpage content, **the complete review form, scoring scale, evaluation criteria, and rating definitions are not included** in this document.\n\nThe page titled \"Instructions for Reviewers\" focuses exclusively on procedural matters, such as:\n\n- Timeline and deadlines for different review phases\n- Bidding periods and paper assignments\n- Discussion and revision periods\n- Quality checks by Area Chairs\n\nThe document emphasizes that reviewers should \"provide constructive feedback to the authors\" and maintain \"active and constructive engagement,\" but it does not specify the actual review form structure, numerical scoring scale, or how different score levels should be interpreted.\n\n**To locate this information, you would need to:**\n- Check the OpenReview platform directly (linked in the navigation)\n- Consult with your assigned Area Chair\n- Review separate reviewer guidelines documents not included in this excerpt"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"44e0fa8a-950a-41be-822f-80b73cae587d","timestamp":"2026-05-15T22:49:59.844Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":124849,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2021 Review Form Information\n\nBased on the provided webpage content, **the complete review form, scoring scale, evaluation criteria, and rating definitions are not included** in this document.\n\nThe page titled \"Instructions for Reviewers\" focuses exclusively on procedural matters, such as:\n\n- Timeline and deadlines for different review phases\n- Bidding periods and paper assignments\n- Discussion and revision periods\n- Quality checks by Area Chairs\n\nThe document emphasizes that reviewers should \"provide constructive feedback to the authors\" and maintain \"active and constructive engagement,\" but it does not specify the actual review form structure, numerical scoring scale, or how different score levels should be interpreted.\n\n**To locate this information, you would need to:**\n- Check the OpenReview platform directly (linked in the navigation)\n- Consult with your assigned Area Chair\n- Review separate reviewer guidelines documents not included in this excerpt","durationMs":3486,"url":"https://2021.corl.org/reviewer-information/instructions-for-reviewers"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YaHYueghTZnK2YkAT77oRj","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Scoring Information\n\nBased on the webpage content provided, here's what can be determined about CoRL 2024 scoring:\n\n## Rating Scale\nThe 2024 conference used a **1-4 point scale** (significantly different from previous years). The data shows: \"min:1.70 max:4.00 avg:3.01 std:0.37\"\n\n## Average Scores for Accepted Papers\nAccepted papers had a mean rating of approximately **3.01** on the 1-4 scale, with scores ranging from 1.70 to 4.00.\n\n## Key Limitation\nThe webpage does **not provide**:\n- Separate statistics comparing accepted versus rejected papers\n- The actual scoring rubric or definitions for each rating level\n- Distribution breakdowns showing how many papers received each score\n\n## Context\nThe acceptance rate was 39.34% (264 of 671 papers accepted). However, the data shows that \"Reject\" counts only include submissions that \"opted in for public release,\" so complete comparative statistics between accepted and rejected submissions are not available on this page.\n\nTo access the detailed scoring rubric and full review distributions, you would need to visit the OpenReview portal directly or contact the conference organizers."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"bd22d2d3-4338-4c6b-bec5-d8f45d7e5285","timestamp":"2026-05-15T22:50:00.201Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":222526,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Scoring Information\n\nBased on the webpage content provided, here's what can be determined about CoRL 2024 scoring:\n\n## Rating Scale\nThe 2024 conference used a **1-4 point scale** (significantly different from previous years). The data shows: \"min:1.70 max:4.00 avg:3.01 std:0.37\"\n\n## Average Scores for Accepted Papers\nAccepted papers had a mean rating of approximately **3.01** on the 1-4 scale, with scores ranging from 1.70 to 4.00.\n\n## Key Limitation\nThe webpage does **not provide**:\n- Separate statistics comparing accepted versus rejected papers\n- The actual scoring rubric or definitions for each rating level\n- Distribution breakdowns showing how many papers received each score\n\n## Context\nThe acceptance rate was 39.34% (264 of 671 papers accepted). However, the data shows that \"Reject\" counts only include submissions that \"opted in for public release,\" so complete comparative statistics between accepted and rejected submissions are not available on this page.\n\nTo access the detailed scoring rubric and full review distributions, you would need to visit the OpenReview portal directly or contact the conference organizers.","durationMs":5241,"url":"https://papercopilot.com/statistics/corl-statistics/corl-2024-statistics/"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TJkCMfLESoMgXfH11uSjtR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've gathered enough information about CoRL reviewer guidelines. Let me summarize what I know:\n\n1. CoRL uses OpenReview\n2. Recent CoRL (2024) used a 1-4 point scale (or it could be 1-10, but the stats show min 1.70 max 4.00 avg 3.01 which could be a 1-4 scale)\n3. Typical acceptance rate: ~39%\n4. Evaluation dimensions from CoRL 2023 guidelines: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n\n\n5. The guidelines emphasize constructive feedback, require a limitations section, and don't mandate SOTA results or real robot demonstrations.\n\nThe 2024 statistics with a maximum of 4.00 and average of 3.01 pretty clearly point to a 1-4 scale, though earlier CoRL editions and other robotics conferences on OpenReview may have used different scales like 1-6 or 1-10. I've settled on a review framework based on CoRL's standard dimensions—Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, and Relevance—with a rating scale that aligns with their recent practices and an expected acceptance rate around 39%. Now I'm ready to dispatch the two reviewers in parallel: one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work coverage. The paper I'm evaluating is about how vision encoders, not language models, drive action-relevant representations in vision-language-action models, submitted to CoRL 2026 as a 7-page submission with multiple tables.\n\nLooking at the key findings, the authors demonstrate that the vision encoder component (specifically SigLIP with a projector) achieves much stronger alignment with action representations than the full VLA model, and they've validated this through RSA and CKA analysis across three LIBERO benchmarks. They also show a consistent per-layer degradation pattern as you move deeper into the language model, and they've controlled for instruction variance through a factorial design. The critical experiments—the per-layer RSA degradation curve and the random instruction control—are both present in the paper, which strengthens their core claims.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":226,"cache_creation_input_tokens":605,"cache_read_input_tokens":54509,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":605},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"4797b623-3a8a-4a8a-a1b8-2c72a51fe4b7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TJkCMfLESoMgXfH11uSjtR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough context on CoRL's review standards. Now dispatching two reviewers in parallel for the full review.\n\n**CoRL review context established:**\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- Typical acceptance rate: ~39% (2021-2024)\n- Recent CoRL uses a condensed rating scale (observed 1-4 in 2024)\n- Required: limitations section, constructive feedback, no strict SOTA requirement\n\nDispatching R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work) now."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":226,"cache_creation_input_tokens":605,"cache_read_input_tokens":54509,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":605},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"683432ff-ea94-49c0-b33e-12d3969356fb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TJkCMfLESoMgXfH11uSjtR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Lw2xX9oxuy5jnTe4rtkkeg","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. \n\n## Venue Context: CoRL 2026\n- Conference on Robot Learning, acceptance rate ~39%\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values: learning relevance to physical robots, technical soundness, well-supported claims\n- Limitations section is required; honestly reported limitations should be treated kindly\n\n## Your Review Focus\nYou are responsible for deeply evaluating:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are RSA and CKA applied correctly? Are the metrics appropriate? Are there methodological gaps?\n2. **Experimental design**: Is the 2×2 factorial design rigorous? Are controls adequate? Are there confounds?\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Are claims well-supported by the numbers? Bootstrap CIs, significance tests, effect sizes?\n4. **Completeness of experiments**: What experiments are missing that should be there?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Vision Encoders, Not Language Models, Drive Action-Relevant Representations in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n**Architecture analyzed**: OpenVLA = SigLIP (frozen vision encoder) + learned projector + Llama-2-7B (33-layer LLM)\n\n**Three claims**:\n1. **Vision Encoder Dominance**: SigLIP+projector RSA ρ=0.615 with action structure, far exceeding full VLA (ρ≈0.488) and DINOv2 (ρ=0.388)\n2. **LLM Degrades Action Alignment**: Per-layer RSA decreases monotonically from projector output through 33 LLM layers (Spearman ρ=−0.88, p=1.2×10⁻¹¹), total drop 0.166. Untrained VLA shows similar trajectory (ρ=−0.82).\n3. **Training and Instruction Invariance**: 2×2 matrix (trained/untrained × with/without instruction) yields RSA in narrow band 0.48-0.49. Even random English, shuffled tokens, pure padding produce no significant change (Δ&lt;0.005).\n\n**Method**: RSA (Spearman rank correlation between pairwise Euclidean distance matrices of representations and actions) and CKA (linear + RBF kernel) as cross-validation metric.\n\n**Key experimental results** (all on LIBERO benchmarks):\n- Table 1: Core RSA on LIBERO-Long. SigLIP+Proj=0.615, Trained+Inst=0.488, Trained-Inst=0.484, Untrained+Inst=0.482, Untrained-Inst=0.479, DINOv2=0.388\n- Table 2: Per-layer RSA degradation. Projector=0.615→L0=0.601→L4=0.571→L8=0.539→L12=0.512→L16=0.496→L20=0.481→L24=0.470→L28=0.458→L32=0.449\n- Table 3: Five instruction conditions all converge in [0.481, 0.489], σ=0.003\n- Table 4: CKA cross-validation preserves same ordering (rank correlation r=0.98 with RSA)\n- Table 5: LIBERO-Spatial replicates all findings\n- Table 6: Cross-benchmark comparison, r=0.96 across 3 LIBERO suites\n\n**Experimental coverage vs. planned experiments**: The paper covers all 4 planned experiments:\n1. Per-layer RSA degradation curve ✓ (Section 4.2)\n2. Random instruction control ✓ (Section 4.3)  \n3. CKA verification ✓ (Section 4.4)\n4. LIBERO-Spatial extension ✓ (Section 4.5)\n\n## What to produce\nWrite a detailed review section covering:\n1. **Summary of methodology assessment** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, 4-6 items focusing on methods/experiments)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, 4-6 items focusing on methods/experiments)\n4. **Questions for authors** (3-5 specific technical questions)\n5. **Minor issues** (if any)\n\nBe specific — cite table numbers, exact values, and section numbers. Be critical but fair. Consider what a skeptical robotics/ML reviewer would ask."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":226,"cache_creation_input_tokens":605,"cache_read_input_tokens":54509,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":605},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"8d4b9564-3709-42ae-8f08-71dca109151f"}
