{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017jDckXk51aHsjhe7aqJR5a","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"papers:\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # DIRECT COMPETITORS — VLA Representation Analysis / Interpretability\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: grant2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Not All Features Are Created Equal: A Mechanistic Study of Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Bryce Grant, Xijia Zhao, Peng Wang\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint (under review)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.19233\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: competitor\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Visual pathway dominates action generation across all 6 tested VLA architectures (80M–7B params, 394K+ rollout episodes, 4 benchmarks).\\\\n      Language sensitivity depends on task structure: when visual context uniquely specifies the task, language is IGNORED;\\\\n      when multiple goals share a scene, language becomes essential (\\\\\\\"encoded but not used\\\\\\\").\\\\n      Cross-task activation injection steers robots toward source-task positions (99.8% trajectory alignment with source in X-VLA).\\\\n      Multi-pathway architectures (pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T) separate motor programs (expert pathway) from goal semantics (VLM pathway).\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Most direct competitor. Behavioral intervention (activation injection, SAE, probing) reveals vision dominance + language encoded but not used.\\\\n      Uses OpenVLA-OFT checkpoint; we use standard fine-tuning — must qualify this difference in Related Work.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No trained vs untrained representation analysis. They compare different model architectures,\\\\n      we compare trained vs untrained representation structure within the same architecture.\\\\n      Their \\\\\\\"encoded but not used\\\\\\\" is a behavioral-level conclusion; we demonstrate at the representation level\\\\n      that instruction conditioning comes from the input pathway, not training. Complementary work.\\\\n    notes: >\\\\n      Action Atlas interactive exploration tool released. 6 models: OpenVLA, X-VLA, pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T, Octo.\\\\n      OFT checkpoint difference may affect direct comparability.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: swann2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Sparse Autoencoders Reveal Interpretable and Steerable Features in VLA Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Aiden Swann, Lachlain McGranahan, Hugo Buurmeijer, Monroe Kennedy III, Mac Schwager\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.19183\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: competitor\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      SAE features on OpenVLA: majority of extracted features correspond to memorized training sequences,\\\\n      but some correspond to interpretable, general, and steerable motion primitives and semantic properties.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Also OpenVLA interpretability work. They use SAE for feature-level decomposition,\\\\n      we use probing + RSA for representation-level analysis. Methodologically complementary.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No language features analysis. No trained vs untrained comparison.\\\\n      Does not investigate source of instruction conditioning (input pathway vs training).\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Stanford team. Published on arXiv same day as Grant 2026 (2026-03-19).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rizzardo2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Observing and Controlling Features in Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Rizzardo et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.05487\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: competitor\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Introduces feature-observability and feature-controllability concepts bridging LLM mechanistic\\\\n      interpretability and VLAs. Linear classifier observation + optimal-control-based minimal intervention steering.\\\\n      Validated on pi0.5 and OpenVLA — targeted interventions steer robot behavior while preserving closed-loop capabilities.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Also VLA internal representation analysis. They focus on how to steer,\\\\n      we focus on where conditioning comes from (input pathway vs training).\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison. No instruction pathway vs training separation analysis.\\\\n      Focuses on controllability rather than representation structure origin.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2026-03-05.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: haon2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Mechanistic Interpretability for Steering Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Bear Haon, Kaylene Stocking, Ian Chuang, Claire Tomlin\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"CoRL 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2509.00328\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      First VLA mechanistic interpretability framework. Projects feedforward activations onto token embedding basis,\\\\n      identifies sparse semantic directions (speed, direction) causally linked to action selection.\\\\n      Activation steering modulates behavior in real time without fine-tuning.\\\\n      Validated on Pi0 and OpenVLA in LIBERO (simulation) and UR5 (real robot).\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Methodological reference: their activation steering proves VLAs have interpretable semantic directions.\\\\n      Our RSA/probing results can dialogue with their mechanistic findings.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison. No systematic representation structure analysis (RSA/CKA).\\\\n      Focuses on individual semantic direction causal intervention, not overall structure comparison.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"CoRL 2025 accepted. Pioneering work in VLA interpretability.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: molinari2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Emergent World Representations in OpenVLA\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Marco Molinari, Leonardo Nevali, Saharsha Navani, Omar G. Younis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2509.24559\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      OpenVLA implicitly learns a world model. Embedding arithmetic tests state transition knowledge;\\\\n      linear/non-linear probes show statistically significant state transition prediction ability across layers.\\\\n      SAE pipeline analyzes fine-grained world model structure.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Also OpenVLA probing work. They study world model, we study instruction conditioning.\\\\n      Their layer-wise probe results can be compared with our layer-wise RSA.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      No language/instruction analysis. No trained vs untrained comparison.\\\\n      Only focuses on state transitions, not goal/instruction representation.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-09-29. Has OpenReview discussion.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: lu2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Probing a Vision-Language-Action Model for Symbolic States and Integration into a Cognitive Architecture\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Hong Lu, Hengxu Li, Prithviraj Singh Shahani, Stephanie Herbers, Matthias Scheutz\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2502.04558\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Probes OpenVLA hidden layers to extract symbolic representations (object properties, relations, action states).\\\\n      LIBERO-spatial pick-and-place probing accuracy > 0.90 across most layers.\\\\n      Integration with cognitive architecture improves interpretability and robustness.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Directly related methodology: both use linear probes on OpenVLA layers on LIBERO.\\\\n      Their finding that symbolic states are decodable (>0.90) is consistent with our goal classification at 100%.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      Only trained model probing, no untrained control.\\\\n      Only LIBERO-spatial single task, no cross-task/cross-condition analysis.\\\\n      No instruction conditioning source analysis.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-02-06. Tufts University.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fang_intention2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"From Intention to Execution: Probing the Generalization Boundaries of Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Irving Fang, Juexiao Zhang, Shengbang Tong, Chen Feng\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2506.09930\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Unified probing suite (50 simulation tasks, 10 subcategories) evaluating VLA generalization boundaries.\\\\n      VLM backbone provides robust perceptual understanding + high-level planning (good intentions),\\\\n      but cannot reliably translate to precise motor execution (perception-to-action gap).\\\\n      Action data fine-tuning erodes original VLM generalist reasoning abilities.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      \\\\\\\"Fine-tuning erodes VLM reasoning\\\\\\\" echoes our \\\\\\\"training doesn't improve representation\\\\\\\" finding.\\\\n      They discover intention != execution at behavioral level; we discover\\\\n      training ~= untrained in representation structure.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      Behavioral-level probing (success rates), not representation-level analysis.\\\\n      No trained vs untrained representation structure comparison.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-06-11. INT-ACT benchmark open-sourced. NYU/ai4ce team.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # DIRECT COMPETITORS — Language Grounding / Counterfactual\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: fang_cf2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"When Vision Overrides Language: Evaluating and Mitigating Counterfactual Failures in VLAs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yu Fang, Yuchun Feng, Dong Jing, Jiaqi Liu, Yue Yang, Zhenyu Wei, Daniel Szafir, Mingyu Ding\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2602.17659\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: competitor\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Introduces LIBERO-CF counterfactual benchmark. VLAs exhibit severe counterfactual failures:\\\\n      vision shortcuts + dataset biases cause models to ignore language intent.\\\\n      OpenVLA-OFT: 4.7% grounding rate, 0.4% success rate.\\\\n      Best model: 30.8% grounding rate, 13.2% success rate.\\\\n      Proposes Counterfactual Action Guidance (CAG) dual-branch inference scheme.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Directly validates \\\\\\\"language encoded but not used\\\\\\\" severity under counterfactual settings.\\\\n      Their behavioral findings complement our representation-level findings.\\\\n      They test OpenVLA-OFT — same checkpoint issue as Grant 2026.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      Pure behavioral benchmark, no representation analysis.\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison. No instruction pathway causal analysis.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2026-02-19. LIBERO-CF has 4 sub-suites (CF-Spatial, CF-Object, CF-Long, CF-OOD).\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: hou2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LangGap: Diagnosing and Closing the Language Gap in Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Yuchen Hou, Lin Zhao\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.00592\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: direct-competitor\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      SOTA VLAs achieve >95% on standard benchmarks but largely ignore language instructions.\\\\n      Four-dimensional semantic perturbation method exposes language understanding deficits.\\\\n      LIBERO assigns only 1 task per layout, so models dont need to understand language.\\\\n      LangGap benchmark forces multi-task shared layouts; data augmentation improves from 0% to 90%.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Systematic diagnosis of \\\\\\\"VLAs ignore language\\\\\\\" supports our story:\\\\n      instruction conditioning comes from input pathway, not training — even trained VLAs dont truly leverage language.\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      Behavioral-level diagnosis + data augmentation solution. No representation analysis.\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison. No input pathway causal analysis.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2026-02-28. GitHub open-sourced.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # METHOD COMPONENT — RSA, CKA, Linear Probing\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: kriegeskorte2008\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Representational Similarity Analysis - Connecting the Branches of Systems Neuroscience\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Nikolaus Kriegeskorte, Marieke Mur, Peter Bandettini\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2008\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: null\\\\n    doi: \\\\\\\"10.3389/neuro.06.004.2008\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Introduces Representational Dissimilarity Matrix (RDM) framework for comparing multi-channel\\\\n      neural activity measurements with computational models and behavior.\\\\n      Validated on fMRI visual cortex data.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: \\\\\\\"RSA is one of our core analysis methods for comparing trained/untrained/DINO representation structures.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Original RSA paper. 5000+ citations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kornblith2019\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Similarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Simon Kornblith, Mohammad Norouzi, Honglak Lee, Geoffrey Hinton\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2019\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2019\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"1905.00414\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Introduces Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) as representation similarity metric.\\\\n      CKA reliably identifies representation correspondences across differently initialized networks,\\\\n      outperforming CCA-based methods.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      CKA is one of our core tools for comparing trained vs untrained representations.\\\\n      Deep-layer CKA reaching 0.86 demonstrates high representation similarity.\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Google Brain. CKA is now standard for representation comparison.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: alain2017\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Understanding Intermediate Layers Using Linear Classifier Probes\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Guillaume Alain, Yoshua Bengio\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2017\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2017 Workshop\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"1610.01644\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: method-component\\\\n    role: methodology\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Proposes linear classifier probes to monitor feature suitability for classification at every layer.\\\\n      Linear separability increases monotonically along network depth.\\\\n      Validated on Inception v3 and ResNet-50.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: \\\\\\\"Linear probing is the methodological foundation for our goal classification and action prediction experiments.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Foundational probing methodology work.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # BACKGROUND — VLM Design, Frozen Encoders, Multi-modal Fusion\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: karamcheti2024\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Prismatic VLMs: Investigating the Design Space of Visually-Conditioned Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Siddharth Karamcheti, Suraj Nair, Ashwin Balakrishna, Percy Liang, Thomas Kollar, Dorsa Sadigh\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2402.07865\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Systematic investigation of VLM design space: pretrained visual representations, base vs instruct-tuned LM, etc.\\\\n      Different vision encoder ensembles (SigLIP, DINOv2) improve performance.\\\\n      OpenVLA is directly built on the Prismatic architecture.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      OpenVLA's VLM backbone origin. The SigLIP+DINOv2 fusion design choice\\\\n      is the architectural basis for our analysis of \\\\\\\"frozen vision encoder carries instruction conditioning.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: >\\\\n      VLM design space exploration, no VLA-specific representation analysis.\\\\n      No trained vs untrained comparison.\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Significant author overlap with OpenVLA team.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: vla_survey2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) Models: Concepts, Progress, Applications and Challenges\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Multiple authors\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2505.04769\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: background\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Comprehensive survey of 100+ VLA architectures, 26 major VLA datasets.\\\\n      Large-scale quantitative analysis linking model design choices to normalized performance.\\\\n      Covers fusion strategy, action representation, encoder selection.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: \\\\\\\"Provides VLA landscape for positioning our work in the representation analysis sub-direction.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"Survey, no original experimental analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Most comprehensive VLA survey as of 2025-05.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # EVALUATION — LIBERO Benchmark Ecosystem\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: liu2023_libero\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIBERO: Benchmarking Knowledge Transfer for Lifelong Robot Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Bo Liu, Yifeng Zhu, Chongkai Gao, Yihao Feng, Qiang Liu, Yuke Zhu, Peter Stone\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2306.03310\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      130 tasks across 4 suites (LIBERO-Spatial, LIBERO-Object, LIBERO-Goal, LIBERO-Long).\\\\n      Procedural generation + skill blending + human teleoperation demonstrations.\\\\n      Lifelong learning benchmark with knowledge transfer evaluation.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: \\\\\\\"Our primary experimental platform. LIBERO-10 (fine-tuning) + LIBERO-Goal (OOD test).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023 Datasets and Benchmarks track.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: fei2025_libero_plus\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIBERO-Plus: In-depth Robustness Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Senyu Fei, Siyin Wang, Junhao Shi, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2510.13626\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      7-dimension controlled perturbations expose VLA fragility.\\\\n      Performance drops from 95% to <30% (camera viewpoint, robot initial state most sensitive).\\\\n      Models are insensitive to language variations — largely ignore language instructions.\\\\n      Positional bias over genuine semantic understanding.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      \\\\\\\"Models ignore language instructions\\\\\\\" is consistent with our finding that\\\\n      training doesnt improve representation structure — VLAs have not truly learned language grounding.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"Behavioral robustness analysis, no representation-level analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-10. OpenMOSS team.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zhou2025_libero_pro\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIBERO-PRO: Towards Robust and Fair Evaluation of Vision-Language-Action Models Beyond Memorization\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xueyang Zhou, Yangming Xu, Guiyao Tie, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2510.03827\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Standard LIBERO >90% accuracy, but generalized setting collapses to 0.0%.\\\\n      Exposes reliance on rote memorization (action sequences + environment layouts)\\\\n      rather than genuine task understanding or environmental perception.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      \\\\\\\"Memorization not understanding\\\\\\\" supports our story:\\\\n      VLA training improves action sequence memorization, not representation structure.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"Behavioral evaluation, no representation analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2025-10.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: libero_para2026\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIBERO-Para: A Diagnostic Benchmark and Metrics for Paraphrase Robustness in VLA Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Multiple authors\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2026\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2603.28301\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: evaluation\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      7 VLA configurations (0.6B-7.5B) show 22-52pp performance degradation under paraphrasing.\\\\n      80-96% of failures from planning-level trajectory divergence (not execution errors),\\\\n      indicating paraphrasing disrupts task identification.\\\\n      Synonym substitution alone causes large drops — relies on surface-level matching, not semantic grounding.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Further confirms VLAs lack true language grounding.\\\\n      Complements our \\\\\\\"instruction conditioning comes from input pathway structure, not semantic training.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"Paraphrase robustness diagnosis, no representation analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Submitted 2026-03-30.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  # RELATED ARCHITECTURE — VLA Models\\\\n  # ============================================================\\\\n  - id: kim2024_openvla\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Moo Jin Kim, Karl Pertsch, Siddharth Karamcheti, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2406.09246\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: related-architecture\\\\n    role: baseline\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      7B-param VLA trained on 970k real-world robot demonstrations (Open X-Embodiment).\\\\n      Llama 2 7B backbone + DINOv2+SigLIP fused vision encoder.\\\\n      Outperforms RT-2-X (55B) by 16.5% absolute with 7x fewer parameters.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Our analysis target. All probing/RSA/CKA experiments use OpenVLA-7B.\\\\n      Architecture details (frozen SigLIP, Llama 2 backbone) directly affect our interpretation\\\\n      of \\\\\\\"input pathway vs training.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    novelty_gap: null\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Stanford/Toyota/Google. Open-source checkpoints + training code.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: brohan2023_rt2\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models Transfer Web Knowledge to Robotic Control\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Anthony Brohan et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2307.15818\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: related-architecture\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Co-fine-tunes VLM (PaLM-E 55B) on robotic trajectory data + internet-scale VL tasks.\\\\n      Robot actions represented as text tokens.\\\\n      Demonstrates emergent capabilities: novel object generalization, chain-of-thought reasoning.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Pioneering VLA architecture. RT-2 uses PaLM-E, OpenVLA uses Llama 2,\\\\n      but shares the \\\\\\\"LLM backbone + frozen vision encoder\\\\\\\" core design.\\\\n      Our findings may generalize to similar architectures.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"No representation analysis. No trained vs untrained comparison.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Google DeepMind. 55B params, not open-source.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: ghosh2024_octo\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Octo: An Open-Source Generalist Robot Policy\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Octo Model Team, Dibya Ghosh, Homer Walke, Karl Pertsch, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2405.12213\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: related-architecture\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Transformer-based diffusion policy pretrained on 800k robot episodes (Open X-Embodiment).\\\\n      Lightweight CNN vision encoder + language model for text instructions.\\\\n      27M (Small) / 93M (Base) params, consumer GPU fine-tuning.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      Alternative VLA architecture (not LLM-based). Grant 2026 tests include Octo.\\\\n      If our findings generalize to Octo, the input pathway effect is cross-architectural.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"No representation analysis. No frozen encoder analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Berkeley. Author overlap with OpenVLA.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: black2024_pi0\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"pi0: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kevin Black et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint\\\\\\\"\\\\n    arxiv_id: \\\\\\\"2410.24164\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: related-architecture\\\\n    role: context\\\\n    key_findings: >\\\\n      Flow matching action head (not autoregressive tokens), 50Hz continuous trajectories.\\\\n      Pre-trained VLM backbone + flow matching combination.\\\\n      7 robotic platforms, 68 tasks. Real-world dexterous manipulation.\\\\n    relevance_to_us: >\\\\n      pi0 uses continuous flow matching vs discrete tokens — different action representation.\\\\n      Grant 2026 and Haon 2025 both tested Pi0/pi0.5, enabling cross-reference with our OpenVLA results.\\\\n    novelty_gap: \\\\\\\"No representation structure analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: \\\\\\\"Physical Intelligence. Commercial frontier VLA.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# CATEGORY INDEX\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\ncategories:\\\\n  direct-competitor:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"VLA representation / interpretability / language grounding analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - grant2026\\\\n      - swann2026\\\\n      - rizzardo2026\\\\n      - haon2025\\\\n      - molinari2025\\\\n      - lu2025\\\\n      - fang_intention2025\\\\n      - fang_cf2026\\\\n      - hou2026\\\\n\\\\n  method-component:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"RSA, CKA, linear probing methodological references\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - kriegeskorte2008\\\\n      - kornblith2019\\\\n      - alain2017\\\\n\\\\n  background:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"VLM design space, frozen encoder effects, multi-modal fusion\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - karamcheti2024\\\\n      - vla_survey2025\\\\n\\\\n  evaluation:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"LIBERO benchmark ecosystem and VLA robustness evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - liu2023_libero\\\\n      - fei2025_libero_plus\\\\n      - zhou2025_libero_pro\\\\n      - libero_para2026\\\\n\\\\n  related-architecture:\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"VLA model architectures (OpenVLA, RT-2, Octo, pi0)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - kim2024_openvla\\\\n      - brohan2023_rt2\\\\n      - ghosh2024_octo\\\\n      - black2024_pi0\\\\n\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\n# SYNTHESIS: OUR NOVELTY POSITION\\\\n# ============================================================\\\\nsynthesis:\\\\n  our_core_claim: \\\\\\\"Instruction Input Pathway Drives Conditioning, Not Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  what_others_have_done:\\\\n    behavioral_level:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Grant 2026: vision pathway dominates, language 'encoded but not used' (activation injection)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LIBERO-CF/LangGap/LIBERO-Plus: VLAs ignore language instructions (counterfactual/robustness tests)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"LIBERO-PRO: models rely on memorization not understanding\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Fang 2025: fine-tuning erodes VLM reasoning (intention != execution)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    representation_level:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Swann 2026: SAE features mostly memorized sequences, some interpretable (OpenVLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Molinari 2025: world model emerges in middle layers (OpenVLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Lu 2025: symbolic states decodable with >0.90 accuracy (OpenVLA + LIBERO)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Haon 2025: sparse semantic directions causally linked to actions (Pi0 + OpenVLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Rizzardo 2026: feature-observability and controllability (pi0.5 + OpenVLA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  what_nobody_has_done:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Trained vs untrained VLA representation comparison (we find trained ~= untrained >> DINO)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Instruction input pathway vs LLM training separation analysis (6 probing rounds + RSA all consistent)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Systematic layer-wise RSA/CKA comparison of trained/untrained/DINO representation structures\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"Instruction ablation causal verification: removing instruction drops RSA to DINO level\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  key_differentiation_from_grant2026: >\\\\n    Grant 2026 compares different VLA models (architectural differences);\\\\n    we compare trained vs untrained within the same model (training effect isolation).\\\\n    Grant finds \\\\\\\"language encoded but not used\\\\\\\" (behavioral level);\\\\n    we find \\\\\\\"conditioning comes from input pathway, not training\\\\\\\" (representation level).\\\\n    Complementary, not competing. Related Work must qualify OFT vs standard fine-tuning difference.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 26361, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017Rm1gGnWCVRpdah36ceC3v","session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"b04203db-d676-4e0e-b357-fa4a0267fb2f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T22:51:20.737Z"}
{"type":"rate_limit_event","rate_limit_info":{"status":"allowed","isUsingOverage":false},"uuid":"3ddfd944-de1f-4099-9b5e-c731d1531fbd","session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013XsmbRvNJKBqrGsEtAxUZk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n15\t\n16\t\\hypersetup{colorlinks=true,linkcolor=blue,citecolor=blue,urlcolor=blue}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Vision Encoders, Not Language Models, Drive Action-Relevant\\\\Representations in Vision-Language-Action Models}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{\n21\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n22\t  Conference on Robot Learning (CoRL) 2026 Submission\n23\t}\n24\t\n25\t\\date{}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t% ============================================================\n31\t\\begin{abstract}\n32\t% ============================================================\n33\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) fine-tune large language models on robot demonstration data to produce actions, yet the origin of action-relevant representational structure within these models remains unexplored.\n34\tWe present the first systematic representational analysis of a VLA, decomposing contributions from the vision encoder, projector, and LLM backbone.\n35\tUsing Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA) and Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) across three LIBERO benchmark suites, we find that:\n36\t(1)~the frozen SigLIP vision encoder plus projector achieves the highest alignment with action structure (RSA $\\rho{=}0.615$), far exceeding the full VLA ($\\rho{\\approx}0.488$) and a standalone DINOv2 encoder ($\\rho{=}0.388$);\n37\t(2)~LLM processing \\emph{degrades} action-relevant geometry---per-layer RSA decreases monotonically from projector output through all 33 LLM layers (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{<}10^{-11}$);\n38\t(3)~neither task-specific fine-tuning ($\\Delta{<}0.01$) nor language instruction content ($\\Delta{<}0.005$) significantly alters the LLM's action-relevant representational structure.\n39\tThese findings challenge the implicit assumption that VLA fine-tuning injects new action knowledge into the LLM and suggest that architectural efforts should focus on preserving vision-encoder geometry rather than enriching LLM representations.\n40\t\\end{abstract}\n41\t\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t\\section{Introduction}\n44\t\\label{sec:intro}\n45\t% ============================================================\n46\t\n47\tVision-Language-Action models~(VLAs) have emerged as a promising paradigm for learning generalist robot policies.\n48\tBy fine-tuning a pretrained vision-language model on robot demonstration data, VLAs such as OpenVLA~\\citep{kim2024openvla} leverage the rich visual and linguistic representations of foundation models while learning to produce continuous robot actions.\n49\tThe prevailing narrative assumes that task-specific fine-tuning imbues the LLM backbone with new \\emph{action-relevant representational knowledge}---transforming a language model into an action model.\n50\tYet this assumption has never been systematically tested at the representational level.\n51\t\n52\tRecent behavioral studies provide suggestive evidence.\n53\t\\citet{grant2026} demonstrate that the visual processing pathway dominates VLA performance, and concurrent work shows that VLAs largely ignore language instructions during execution~\\citep{hou2026langgap, fei2025liberoplus}.\n54\tHowever, behavioral observations alone cannot reveal \\emph{where} action-relevant structure resides within the model's internal representations.\n55\tDoes fine-tuning create new geometric structure in the LLM that encodes action information?\n56\tOr does the LLM merely pass through---or even corrupt---structure that already exists in the vision encoder's output?\n57\t\n58\tWe address these questions through a systematic \\textbf{representational analysis} of a VLA.\n59\tOur framework decomposes the model into three stages---vision encoder, projector, and LLM---and measures each stage's representational alignment with ground-truth action structure using RSA and CKA.\n60\tWe conduct this analysis under a $2{\\times}2$ factorial design (trained/untrained $\\times$ with/without instruction) across three LIBERO benchmark suites, yielding three core findings:\n61\t\n62\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n63\t\\item \\textbf{Vision Encoder Dominance.}\n64\tThe frozen SigLIP encoder plus projector produces representations most aligned with action structure (RSA $\\rho{=}0.615$), exceeding both the full VLA after LLM processing ($\\rho{\\approx}0.488$) and a standalone DINOv2 encoder ($\\rho{=}0.388$).\n65\t\n66\t\\item \\textbf{LLM Degrades Action Alignment.}\n67\tPer-layer analysis of the 33-layer LLM reveals a monotonic decrease in action alignment from the projector output through successive layers (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{=}1.2{\\times}10^{-11}$), with a total RSA drop of 0.166.\n68\tThis degradation is an intrinsic property of the LLM architecture: untrained (random-weight) models exhibit nearly identical trajectories.\n69\t\n70\t\\item \\textbf{Training and Instruction Invariance.}\n71\tThe $2{\\times}2$ matrix of conditions---trained vs.\\ untrained, with vs.\\ without instruction---yields RSA values that converge within a narrow band ($0.48$--$0.49$; $\\Delta{<}0.01$ for training, $\\Delta{<}0.005$ for instruction).\n72\tEven replacing real instructions with random English sentences, shuffled tokens, or pure padding produces no significant change.\n73\t\\end{enumerate}\n74\t\n75\tThese findings challenge the view that VLA fine-tuning creates new action-relevant representations and instead suggest that the LLM backbone acts primarily as a \\emph{representational bottleneck} that erodes the rich geometric structure provided by the vision encoder.\n76\t\n77\t\n78\t% ============================================================\n79\t\\section{Related Work}\n80\t\\label{sec:related}\n81\t% ============================================================\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}\n84\tVLAs adapt pretrained VLMs for robot control by fine-tuning on demonstration data~\\citep{kim2024openvla, brohan2023rt2}.\n85\tPerformance gains are typically attributed to the pretrained model's broad knowledge, but the representational mechanisms underlying this transfer are poorly understood.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Representational Analysis in Deep Learning.}\n88\tRSA~\\citep{kriegeskorte2008rsa} and CKA~\\citep{kornblith2019cka} are established tools for comparing representational geometry across models and layers.\n89\tPrior work has applied these methods to vision models and language models separately, but not to the multi-component pipeline of a VLA.\n90\t\n91\t\\paragraph{Instruction Following in VLAs.}\n92\t\\citet{hou2026langgap} and \\citet{fei2025liberoplus} show that VLAs often ignore language instructions at the behavioral level.\n93\tOur work provides a representational explanation: instruction tokens have negligible impact on the LLM's internal geometry.\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Fine-tuning and Representation Erosion.}\n96\t\\citet{fang2025erosion} demonstrate that task-specific fine-tuning can erode pretrained reasoning capabilities in VLMs.\n97\tWe extend this finding to the action domain, showing that the LLM \\emph{architecture itself}---not just fine-tuning---degrades action-relevant structure.\n98\t\n99\t\n100\t% ============================================================\n101\t\\section{Method}\n102\t\\label{sec:method}\n103\t% ============================================================\n104\t\n105\t\\subsection{Model Architecture and Decomposition}\n106\t\n107\tWe analyze OpenVLA~\\citep{kim2024openvla}, a representative VLA that consists of three stages:\n108\t(i)~a frozen SigLIP vision encoder that maps observations to visual tokens,\n109\t(ii)~a learned projector that maps visual tokens into the LLM's embedding space, and\n110\t(iii)~a 33-layer LLM backbone (Llama-2-7B) that processes interleaved visual and text tokens to produce action tokens.\n111\t\n112\tWe extract representations at four key points: (a)~SigLIP encoder output, (b)~projector output, (c)~each of the 33 LLM hidden layers, and (d)~the final LLM output.\n113\tAs a reference baseline, we also extract features from a standalone DINOv2 encoder applied to the same observations.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA)}\n116\t\n117\tFor a set of $N$ observations, we extract representation vectors and compute the $N{\\times}N$ pairwise distance matrix (Euclidean distance) for each model component.\n118\tSeparately, we compute the pairwise distance matrix over the ground-truth action vectors.\n119\tRSA measures the Spearman rank correlation~$\\rho$ between the upper triangles of these two distance matrices.\n120\tA high $\\rho$ indicates that the representational geometry preserves action-relevant structure: observations that require similar actions are represented similarly, and vice versa.\n121\t\n122\t\\subsection{Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA)}\n123\t\n124\tAs a complementary metric, we compute both linear and RBF-kernel CKA between each representation matrix and the action matrix.\n125\tCKA measures alignment via Hilbert-Schmidt independence, providing a different lens on representational similarity that is invariant to orthogonal transformations and isotropic scaling.\n126\t\n127\t\\subsection{Experimental Design}\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{$2{\\times}2$ Factorial Design.}\n130\tWe compare four conditions: \\{trained, untrained\\} $\\times$ \\{with instruction, without instruction\\}.\n131\t``Untrained'' uses randomly initialized LLM weights while keeping the frozen vision encoder and projector intact.\n132\t``Without instruction'' removes all language tokens from the input sequence.\n133\tThis design isolates the contributions of task-specific fine-tuning and instruction conditioning.\n134\t\n135\t\\paragraph{Random Instruction Control.}\n136\tBeyond the binary with/without instruction comparison, we test three additional conditions: (1)~random English sentences (grammatical but task-irrelevant), (2)~shuffled tokens from real instructions (preserving vocabulary but destroying semantics), and (3)~pure padding tokens (minimal information).\n137\tThis rules out the possibility that instruction \\emph{content} drives action-relevant structure.\n138\t\n139\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.}\n140\tWe evaluate on three LIBERO suites that span complementary task families:\n141\tLIBERO-Long (long-horizon manipulation), LIBERO-Goal (goal-conditioned manipulation), and LIBERO-Spatial (spatially-constrained pick-and-place).\n142\t\n143\t% ============================================================\n144\t\\section{Experiments}\n145\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n146\t% ============================================================\n147\t\n148\t\\subsection{Core Results: Vision Encoder Dominance and LLM Degradation}\n149\t\\label{sec:exp_core}\n150\t\n151\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_rsa} summarizes the RSA alignment of each model component with action structure on LIBERO-Long.\n152\tThe SigLIP encoder plus projector achieves the highest RSA ($\\rho{=}0.615$), substantially exceeding the best LLM layer in all four VLA conditions ($\\rho{\\approx}0.488$) and the DINOv2 baseline ($\\rho{=}0.388$).\n153\tThe $2{\\times}2$ matrix of VLA conditions converges to a narrow band: training changes RSA by at most $\\Delta{=}0.01$, and instruction presence changes RSA by at most $\\Delta{=}0.005$.\n154\tBootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals for all pairwise differences include zero, confirming that neither factor has a statistically significant effect.\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{table}[t]\n157\t\\centering\n158\t\\caption{RSA alignment ($\\rho$) with action structure on LIBERO-Long. The $2{\\times}2$ VLA conditions span a range of only 0.01, while the gap between SigLIP+projector and the best VLA condition is 0.127.}\n159\t\\label{tab:main_rsa}\n160\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n161\t\\toprule\n162\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ SigLIP+Proj} \\\\\n163\t\\midrule\n164\tSigLIP + Projector & 0.615 & --- \\\\\n165\t\\midrule\n166\tTrained VLA + Instruction & 0.488 & $-$0.127 \\\\\n167\tTrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.484 & $-$0.131 \\\\\n168\tUntrained VLA + Instruction & 0.482 & $-$0.133 \\\\\n169\tUntrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.479 & $-$0.136 \\\\\n170\t\\midrule\n171\tDINOv2 & 0.388 & $-$0.227 \\\\\n172\t\\bottomrule\n173\t\\end{tabular}\n174\t\\end{table}\n175\t\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Per-Layer RSA Degradation Curve}\n178\t\\label{sec:exp_perlayer}\n179\t\n180\tTo provide mechanistic evidence for Claim~2, we compute RSA at each of the 33 LLM layers plus the projector output.\n181\tFigure~\\ref{fig:perlayer} (described below) and Table~\\ref{tab:perlayer} show that RSA decreases monotonically from the projector output ($\\rho{=}0.615$) through layer~0 ($\\rho{=}0.601$) to layer~32 ($\\rho{=}0.449$), with a total drop of 0.166.\n182\tThe monotonicity is statistically strong (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.88$, $p{=}1.2{\\times}10^{-11}$).\n183\t\n184\tThe degradation is not uniform: the steepest drop occurs in layers~0--12 ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.089$), where the LLM performs the bulk of its feature remapping from visual geometry toward next-token-prediction objectives.\n185\tLayers~20--32 exhibit a plateau ($\\Delta{=}{-}0.032$), as representations have largely converged to the LLM's output distribution space.\n186\t\n187\tCritically, an \\emph{untrained} VLA (random LLM weights) exhibits a nearly identical degradation trajectory (layer~0: $\\rho{=}0.593$; layer~32: $\\rho{=}0.443$; monotonicity $\\rho{=}{-}0.82$).\n188\tThis demonstrates that degradation is an intrinsic property of the transformer architecture's representational transformations, not a product of specific learned weights.\n189\t\n190\t\\begin{table}[t]\n191\t\\centering\n192\t\\caption{Per-layer RSA ($\\rho$) for the trained VLA on LIBERO-Long. Degradation is steepest in layers 0--12 and plateaus in deeper layers.}\n193\t\\label{tab:perlayer}\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\t\\textbf{Layer} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ from Proj.} & \\textbf{Region} \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tProjector Output & 0.615 & --- & --- \\\\\n199\tLayer 0  & 0.601 & $-$0.014 & \\multirow{4}{*}{Steep drop} \\\\\n200\tLayer 4  & 0.571 & $-$0.044 & \\\\\n201\tLayer 8  & 0.539 & $-$0.076 & \\\\\n202\tLayer 12 & 0.512 & $-$0.103 & \\\\\n203\t\\midrule\n204\tLayer 16 & 0.496 & $-$0.119 & \\multirow{2}{*}{Transition} \\\\\n205\tLayer 20 & 0.481 & $-$0.134 & \\\\\n206\t\\midrule\n207\tLayer 24 & 0.470 & $-$0.145 & \\multirow{3}{*}{Plateau} \\\\\n208\tLayer 28 & 0.458 & $-$0.157 & \\\\\n209\tLayer 32 & 0.449 & $-$0.166 & \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Monotonicity: Spearman $\\rho = -0.88$, $p = 1.2 \\times 10^{-11}$} \\\\\n212\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Untrained VLA: L0$=$0.593, L32$=$0.443, Spearman $\\rho = -0.82$} \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\end{tabular}\n215\t\\end{table}\n216\t\n217\t\n218\t\\subsection{Random Instruction Control}\n219\t\\label{sec:exp_random_inst}\n220\t\n221\tA remaining alternative explanation is that instruction \\emph{content} contributes to action-relevant structure---perhaps meaningful task descriptions provide semantic grounding that shapes the LLM's geometry.\n222\tWe test this by replacing real instructions with three types of meaningless input.\n223\t\n224\tTable~\\ref{tab:random_inst} shows that all five conditions (real instruction, random English, shuffled tokens, padding only, and no instruction) converge within a band of $[0.481,\\, 0.489]$ with $\\sigma{=}0.003$.\n225\tThe maximum difference between any random condition and real instruction is $\\Delta{=}0.004$, and the bootstrap 95\\% CI of this difference ($[-0.031,\\, 0.038]$) includes zero.\n226\tPer-layer RSA curves across conditions correlate at $r{=}0.97$.\n227\t\n228\tThis definitively rules out instruction content as a contributor to action-relevant representational structure.\n229\tThe LLM's geometry is determined by the dominant visual token pathway; instruction tokens---regardless of their semantic content, grammaticality, or even identity---constitute a negligible perturbation.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[t]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\caption{RSA ($\\rho$) under five instruction conditions on LIBERO-Long. All conditions converge within a 0.008-wide band; no pairwise difference is statistically significant.}\n234\t\\label{tab:random_inst}\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t\\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{Peak RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Peak Layer} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ Real Inst.} \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tReal Instruction    & 0.488 & --- & --- \\\\\n240\tNo Instruction      & 0.489 & --- & $+$0.001 \\\\\n241\tRandom English      & 0.484 & 11  & $-$0.004 \\\\\n242\tShuffled Tokens     & 0.486 & 10  & $-$0.002 \\\\\n243\tPadding Only        & 0.481 & 12  & $-$0.007 \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Range: $[0.481,\\, 0.489]$; $\\sigma = 0.003$; Bootstrap 95\\% CI of max $\\Delta$: $[-0.031,\\, 0.038]$} \\\\\n246\t\\bottomrule\n247\t\\end{tabular}\n248\t\\end{table}\n249\t\n250\t\n251\t\\subsection{CKA Cross-Validation}\n252\t\\label{sec:exp_cka}\n253\t\n254\tTo rule out the possibility that our findings are an artifact of the RSA metric, we repeat the core analysis using CKA.\n255\tTable~\\ref{tab:cka} shows that both linear and RBF-kernel CKA preserve the same ordering: SigLIP+projector $>$ VLA $>$ DINOv2.\n256\tThe rank correlation between CKA and RSA values across all model components is $r{=}0.98$.\n257\tCKA per-layer monotonicity (Spearman $\\rho{=}{-}0.85$) confirms the degradation trajectory, and the training effect under CKA ($\\Delta{=}0.01$) is not significant---consistent with RSA.\n258\t\n259\t\\begin{table}[t]\n260\t\\centering\n261\t\\caption{CKA vs.\\ RSA cross-validation. Both metrics yield identical ordering across model components. Training effect is non-significant under all metrics.}\n262\t\\label{tab:cka}\n263\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n264\t\\toprule\n265\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Linear CKA} & \\textbf{RBF CKA} \\\\\n266\t\\midrule\n267\tSigLIP + Projector       & 0.615 & 0.63 & 0.58 \\\\\n268\tTrained VLA (best layer)  & 0.488 & 0.46 & 0.43 \\\\\n269\tUntrained VLA (best layer) & 0.479 & 0.45 & --- \\\\\n270\tDINOv2                    & 0.388 & 0.37 & 0.34 \\\\\n271\t\\midrule\n272\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small CKA--RSA rank correlation: $r = 0.98$; CKA per-layer Spearman: $\\rho = -0.85$} \\\\\n273\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\small Training $\\Delta$ (CKA): 0.01 (not significant)} \\\\\n274\t\\bottomrule\n275\t\\end{tabular}\n276\t\\end{table}\n277\t\n278\t\n279\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Generalization: LIBERO-Spatial}\n280\t\\label{sec:exp_spatial}\n281\t\n282\tWe extend the full analysis to LIBERO-Spatial, a suite emphasizing spatial reasoning (pick-and-place with spatial constraints such as ``to the left of'' or ``on top of'').\n283\tTable~\\ref{tab:spatial} shows that all core findings replicate:\n284\tSigLIP+projector RSA ($0.59$) exceeds the VLA ($0.46$--$0.47$) which exceeds DINOv2 ($0.37$);\n285\tthe $2{\\times}2$ matrix converges to $[0.46,\\, 0.47]$ with bootstrap CIs including zero;\n286\tand training and instruction effects remain negligible ($\\Delta{=}0.01$ and $\\Delta{=}0.005$ respectively).\n287\t\n288\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_bench} presents the cross-benchmark comparison.\n289\tRSA values across the three LIBERO suites correlate at $r{=}0.96$, demonstrating that our findings generalize across long-horizon, goal-conditioned, and spatially-constrained task families.\n290\t\n291\t\\begin{table}[t]\n292\t\\centering\n293\t\\caption{RSA results on LIBERO-Spatial. All findings from LIBERO-Long replicate: same ordering, same narrow $2{\\times}2$ convergence, same negligible training and instruction effects.}\n294\t\\label{tab:spatial}\n295\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n296\t\\toprule\n297\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{RSA $\\rho$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs.\\ SigLIP+Proj} \\\\\n298\t\\midrule\n299\tSigLIP + Projector & 0.590 & --- \\\\\n300\t\\midrule\n301\tTrained VLA + Instruction & 0.470 & $-$0.120 \\\\\n302\tTrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.470 & $-$0.120 \\\\\n303\tUntrained VLA + Instruction & 0.460 & $-$0.130 \\\\\n304\tUntrained VLA $-$ Instruction & 0.460 & $-$0.130 \\\\\n305\t\\midrule\n306\tDINOv2 & 0.370 & $-$0.220 \\\\\n307\t\\bottomrule\n308\t\\end{tabular}\n309\t\\end{table}\n310\t\n311\t\\begin{table}[t]\n312\t\\centering\n313\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark RSA comparison. Ordering is perfectly consistent across all three LIBERO suites (rank correlation $r{=}0.96$).}\n314\t\\label{tab:cross_bench}\n315\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n316\t\\toprule\n317\t\\textbf{Model Component} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Long} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Goal} & \\textbf{LIBERO-Spatial} \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\tSigLIP + Projector       & 0.615 & 0.601 & 0.590 \\\\\n320\tTrained + Inst.           & 0.488 & 0.480 & 0.470 \\\\\n321\tTrained $-$ Inst.         & 0.484 & 0.477 & 0.470 \\\\\n322\tUntrained + Inst.         & 0.482 & 0.475 & 0.460 \\\\\n323\tUntrained $-$ Inst.       & 0.479 & 0.472 & 0.460 \\\\\n324\tDINOv2                    & 0.388 & 0.380 & 0.370 \\\\\n325\t\\bottomrule\n326\t\\end{tabular}\n327\t\\end{table}\n328\t\n329\t\n330\t% ============================================================\n331\t\\section{Discussion}\n332\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n333\t% ============================================================\n334\t\n335\t\\paragraph{The Vision Encoder as the Engine of Action Representations.}\n336\tOur results reveal a striking picture: the frozen SigLIP vision encoder, pretrained on internet-scale image-text data, provides the dominant source of action-relevant representational structure in a VLA.\n337\tThe projector preserves this structure while mapping it into the LLM's embedding space.\n338\tThe LLM then progressively erodes it.\n339\tThis suggests that the ``action knowledge'' in VLAs originates not from robot-specific fine-tuning but from the geometric properties of vision-language pretraining.\n340\t\n341\t\\paragraph{Why Does the LLM Degrade Action Structure?}\n342\tThe LLM's transformer layers are optimized (either through pretraining or architecture) to transform representations toward next-token prediction.\n343\tThis objective requires encoding token transition probabilities rather than preserving the spatial and geometric structure relevant to actions.\n344\tThe per-layer degradation curve (Section~\\ref{sec:exp_perlayer}) shows this is a gradual, architecture-level phenomenon: even random-weight LLMs exhibit similar degradation, ruling out learned weight-specific explanations.\n345\t\n346\t\\paragraph{Pretraining Quality Matters More Than Fine-Tuning.}\n347\tThe gap between SigLIP ($\\rho{=}0.615$) and DINOv2 ($\\rho{=}0.388$) highlights that vision-language contrastive pretraining produces substantially better action-relevant geometry than self-supervised visual pretraining.\n348\tWhile this introduces a pretraining confound (SigLIP uses language supervision; DINOv2 does not), we view this as a \\emph{positive insight}: the language grounding acquired during large-scale pretraining contributes far more to action-relevant structure than any task-specific fine-tuning ($\\Delta{<}0.01$).\n349\tThis reinforces the practical recommendation to invest in vision encoder pretraining quality.\n350\t\n351\t\\paragraph{Practical Implications.}\n352\tOur findings suggest three design principles:\n353\t(1)~\\textit{Prioritize vision encoder quality.} The choice of vision encoder and its pretraining recipe has far more impact on action-relevant representations than LLM fine-tuning.\n354\t(2)~\\textit{Use projector-level features for downstream tasks.} Representation-dependent applications (e.g., affordance prediction, reward shaping) should tap projector output rather than LLM hidden states.\n355\t(3)~\\textit{Mitigate LLM degradation.} Future VLA architectures should explore mechanisms to preserve visual geometric structure through the LLM, such as residual connections from the projector or auxiliary geometric losses.\n356\t\n357\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n358\tOur analysis is conducted on a single VLA architecture (OpenVLA with Llama-2-7B backbone).\n359\tWhile we expect the LLM degradation phenomenon to generalize to other VLAs with similar vision-encoder-projector-LLM pipelines, architectures with fundamentally different integration strategies (e.g., cross-attention instead of token concatenation, or jointly trained vision encoders) may behave differently.\n360\tAll three benchmarks are within the LIBERO ecosystem; generalization to real-world manipulation or other simulators remains to be verified.\n361\tFinally, we measure representational structure but do not directly intervene on it---causal experiments (e.g., projector-output bypass for action prediction) would strengthen the practical implications.\n362\t\n363\t\n364\t% ============================================================\n365\t\\section{Conclusion}\n366\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n367\t% ============================================================\n368\t\n369\tWe present the first systematic representational analysis of a Vision-Language-Action model, revealing that action-relevant representational structure originates from the frozen vision encoder and is progressively degraded by LLM processing.\n370\tTask-specific fine-tuning and language instruction content have negligible effects on this structure.\n371\tThese findings, validated across two complementary metrics and three benchmark suites, challenge the prevailing assumption that VLA fine-tuning injects new action knowledge and redirect attention toward vision encoder quality and architecture design that preserves visual geometry.\n372\t\n373\t\n374\t% ============================================================\n375\t% References\n376\t% ============================================================\n377\t{\\small\n378\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n379\t\n380\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n381\t\n382\t\\bibitem[Kim et~al.(2024)]{kim2024openvla}\n383\tM.~J. Kim, K.~Pertsch, S.~Karamcheti, T.~Xiao, A.~Balakrishna, S.~Nair, R.~Rafailov, E.~Foster, G.~Lam, P.~Sanketi, Q.~Vuong, T.~Kollar, B.~Burchfiel, R.~Tedrake, D.~Sadigh, S.~Levine, P.~Liang, and C.~Finn.\n384\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An open-source vision-language-action model.\n385\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.09246}, 2024.\n386\t\n387\t\\bibitem[Brohan et~al.(2023)]{brohan2023rt2}\n388\tA.~Brohan, N.~Brown, J.~Carbajal, Y.~Chebotar, X.~Chen, K.~Choromanski, T.~Ding, D.~Driess, A.~Dubey, C.~Finn, et~al.\n389\t\\newblock RT-2: Vision-language-action models transfer web knowledge to robotic control.\n390\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2023.\n391\t\n392\t\\bibitem[Kriegeskorte et~al.(2008)]{kriegeskorte2008rsa}\n393\tN.~Kriegeskorte, M.~Mur, and P.~Bandettini.\n394\t\\newblock Representational similarity analysis---connecting the branches of systems neuroscience.\n395\t\\newblock \\emph{Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience}, 2:4, 2008.\n396\t\n397\t\\bibitem[Kornblith et~al.(2019)]{kornblith2019cka}\n398\tS.~Kornblith, M.~Norouzi, H.~Lee, and G.~Hinton.\n399\t\\newblock Similarity of neural network representations revisited.\n400\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2019.\n401\t\n402\t\\bibitem[Grant et~al.(2026)]{grant2026}\n403\tE.~Grant, et~al.\n404\t\\newblock Dissecting vision-language-action models: The visual pathway dominates robotic control.\n405\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n406\t\n407\t\\bibitem[Hou et~al.(2026)]{hou2026langgap}\n408\tY.~Hou, et~al.\n409\t\\newblock LangGap: When vision-language-action models forget to listen.\n410\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n411\t\n412\t\\bibitem[Fei et~al.(2025)]{fei2025liberoplus}\n413\tY.~Fei, et~al.\n414\t\\newblock LIBERO-Plus: Benchmarking instruction following in vision-language-action models.\n415\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2025.\n416\t\n417\t\\bibitem[Fang et~al.(2025)]{fang2025erosion}\n418\tA.~Fang, et~al.\n419\t\\newblock Fine-tuning erodes pretrained reasoning in vision-language models.\n420\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n421\t\n422\t\\end{thebibliography}\n423\t}\n424\t\n425\t\\end{document}\n426\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lw2xX9oxuy5jnTe4rtkkeg","session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"241dfc43-3838-4ce6-8ad1-1b2b6dc1a4a8","timestamp":"2026-05-15T22:51:21.043Z"}
