{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017Rm1gGnWCVRpdah36ceC3v","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me synthesize my review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty/Contribution and Related Work/Positioning\n\n### 1. Summary of Novelty/Contribution Assessment\n\nThis paper applies standard representational analysis tools (RSA, CKA) to a new domain — the internal pipeline of a VLA — and decomposes where action-relevant structure resides. The core finding that the frozen vision encoder dominates and the LLM degrades action alignment is a genuinely useful empirical contribution, but the novelty is substantially diminished by concurrent work (Grant et al. 2026, Swann et al. 2026, Haon 2025) that reached similar high-level conclusions through behavioral and mechanistic methods. The paper's unique value is the trained-vs-untrained representational comparison and the quantitative component-level decomposition, which no prior work has done — but the insight is more confirmatory than surprising, and the practical implications remain speculative without causal validation.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-designed factorial experiment.** The 2x2 (trained/untrained x with/without instruction) design is clean and isolates variables that matter. The addition of random/shuffled/padding instruction controls is thorough and pre-empts a natural objection about instruction content.\n\n- **Quantitative component-level decomposition is genuinely novel.** No prior work has measured RSA alignment with action structure at the projector output vs. LLM hidden states vs. standalone vision encoder. The SigLIP+projector RSA of 0.615 vs. full VLA 0.488 is a concrete, interpretable result that adds precision to the \"vision dominates\" narrative established behaviorally by Grant et al.\n\n- **Methodological rigor in aggregation robustness.** The authors identified a potential aggregation artifact (SigLIP mean pooling vs. VLA last-preaction token) and verified Claim 2 holds under matched aggregation (image_mean: 0.501 vs. 0.615). This shows careful experimental hygiene.\n\n- **Converging evidence across methods and benchmarks.** Seven probing methods, two metrics (RSA and CKA), and multiple LIBERO suites all point in the same direction. The internal consistency is high: 23 successful experiments, 7 negative, 0 failed — the negative results are informative (instruction ablation, output head analysis) and honestly reported.\n\n- **Clear writing and well-scoped claims.** The paper explicitly limits scope to \"OpenVLA-class architectures\" and acknowledges the single-architecture limitation. The paper_framing.md shows the authors anticipated reviewer concerns (C1-C6) and prepared responses, suggesting mature scientific judgment.\n\n- **The per-layer degradation curve is an informative contribution.** Showing that degradation concentrates at the Proj-to-L1 interface (drop of 0.13-0.25) with partial non-monotonic recovery in intermediate layers adds mechanistic nuance beyond a simple two-point comparison. The finding that even random-weight LLMs show similar degradation is striking.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **The core finding is largely confirmatory of concurrent work.** Grant et al. (2026) already demonstrated across 6 VLA architectures that the visual pathway dominates action generation. Hou (2026), Fei (2025), Fang et al. (2026), and others showed VLAs ignore language instructions. This paper moves the evidence from behavioral to representational level, which is a step forward, but the headline conclusion — \"vision encoder matters most, LLM and instructions don't\" — is not new. For CoRL, the novelty bar requires that the representational-level evidence provide insights not obtainable from behavioral studies. The paper argues this but the actual new insights (LLM degrades rather than passes through; degradation is architecture-intrinsic) are narrower than the framing suggests.\n\n- **Single architecture severely limits generalizability of the strongest claims.** The paper studies only OpenVLA (SigLIP + Llama-2-7B with token concatenation). Architecturally different VLAs — pi0/pi0.5 (flow matching action head), GR00T (multi-pathway), models using cross-attention fusion, or jointly trained vision encoders — may behave entirely differently. The claim \"LLM degrades action alignment\" may be specific to prefix-tuning architectures where visual tokens are simply prepended. Cross-attention architectures, for example, could allow the LLM to selectively attend to action-relevant visual features without disrupting their geometry. The paper acknowledges this but the title and abstract make universal-sounding claims (\"Vision Encoders, Not Language Models, Drive...\") that are not supported by single-architecture evidence.\n\n- **The \"untrained\" control conflates two things.** The \"untrained\" model uses randomly initialized LLM weights but keeps the frozen SigLIP encoder and projector intact. This means the comparison is really \"pretrained+finetuned LLM vs. random LLM, both with identical vision pipeline.\" Since the vision pipeline is frozen and identical in both cases, finding that representations are similar is partly tautological — the dominant signal source (256 visual tokens) is literally the same. The paper should more explicitly acknowledge that this design cannot distinguish \"LLM contributes nothing\" from \"LLM contributions are orthogonal to what RSA/CKA measure.\" The LLM may contribute action-relevant structure in dimensions that RSA (based on Euclidean pairwise distances) does not capture well, e.g., discrete mode selection or sequential dependencies.\n\n- **No causal intervention on representations.** RSA measures correlation between representational geometry and action structure, but correlation is not causation. The paper cannot rule out that the LLM transforms representations in ways that are critical for action decoding even if they reduce RSA alignment. A model that \"degrades\" RSA might still be essential for the action head to decode actions correctly. The missing experiment is straightforward: feed projector output directly to the action head (bypassing the LLM) and measure task success. If projector-level features yield comparable performance, the \"degradation\" claim gains causal force. Without this, the practical implications (Section: \"use projector-level features for downstream tasks\") are unsupported speculation.\n\n- **LIBERO ecosystem limitations are more serious than acknowledged.** All benchmarks are within LIBERO — same simulator, same robot morphology (Franka Panda), same object set, same camera setup. LIBERO tasks have known issues: single task per layout (Hou 2026), heavy reliance on memorized trajectories (Zhou 2025), and vision-solvability for most tasks (Grant 2026). The finding that \"instruction doesn't matter\" may be a LIBERO artifact rather than a VLA property. The paper should test at minimum one non-LIBERO benchmark or explicitly discuss why LIBERO-specific biases (vision-solvability, one-task-per-layout) might inflate the vision dominance result.\n\n- **The per-layer curve description is inconsistent between paper versions and actual data.** The project memory records that the degradation is \"non-monotonic, concentrated at embedding interface\" with partial recovery in middle layers and a no-inst trained condition showing L1-to-L30 continuous increase. Yet the best-case paper draft claims \"monotonic decrease\" with Spearman rho = -0.88. The median draft correctly reports non-monotonicity (rho = -0.62, 8 violations). This inconsistency between the actual experimental findings and how they are reported is a serious concern. The authors should decide which narrative the data actually supports and present it honestly.\n\n### 4. Missing Related Work\n\nThe following papers/areas should be cited or discussed:\n\n- **Rizzardo et al. (2026), \"Observing and Controlling Features in VLAs\" (arXiv 2603.05487)**: Directly relevant VLA interpretability work on pi0.5 and OpenVLA using feature-observability and controllability frameworks. Published before this submission. Omission is notable given identical model and domain.\n\n- **Haon et al. (2025), \"Mechanistic Interpretability for Steering VLAs\" (CoRL 2025)**: First VLA mechanistic interpretability work, accepted at CoRL 2025 — the same venue this paper targets. Projects feedforward activations onto semantic directions, demonstrates causal activation steering on OpenVLA in LIBERO. Must be cited and differentiated.\n\n- **Molinari et al. (2025), \"Emergent World Representations in OpenVLA\"**: Probing work on the same model (OpenVLA) showing layer-wise world model emergence. Their per-layer probe results should be compared with this paper's per-layer RSA.\n\n- **Lu et al. (2025), \"Probing a VLA for Symbolic States\"**: Linear probing on OpenVLA hidden layers in LIBERO with >0.90 accuracy for symbolic states. Methodologically closest prior work — same model, same benchmark, same technique family.\n\n- **Swann & Buurmeijer (2026), \"Sparse Autoencoders Reveal Interpretable Features in VLA Models\"**: SAE-based OpenVLA interpretability published concurrently (same arXiv day as Grant 2026). Must be discussed as concurrent work.\n\n- **Fang et al. (2026), \"When Vision Overrides Language\" (LIBERO-CF)**: Introduces counterfactual LIBERO benchmark showing VLAs exhibit severe counterfactual failures. OpenVLA-OFT 4.7% grounding rate. Directly supports the \"VLAs ignore language\" narrative with controlled experiments.\n\n- **LIBERO-Para (2026, arXiv 2603.28301)**: Shows 22-52pp degradation under paraphrasing, indicating surface-level matching rather than semantic grounding. Supports instruction invariance claim.\n\n- **Karamcheti et al. (2024), \"Prismatic VLMs\"**: OpenVLA's architectural foundation. The SigLIP+DINOv2 fusion design choice is directly relevant to understanding why the vision encoder carries action structure.\n\n- **The broader literature on frozen encoder effectiveness in VLMs** (e.g., LLaVA family, Frozen in Time, etc.) should be discussed to contextualize whether \"frozen encoder dominates\" is a VLA-specific finding or a general property of adapter-style multimodal architectures.\n\n### 5. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the \"degradation\" framing**: RSA measures geometric alignment between representation space and action space, but the LLM's job is to produce correct action *tokens*, not to preserve geometric structure. Have you verified that the RSA \"degradation\" actually corresponds to worse downstream performance? Specifically, if you train a linear action predictor on projector output vs. final LLM hidden states, does projector output yield better action prediction? If the LLM hidden states yield better action prediction despite lower RSA, the \"degradation\" narrative would need fundamental revision.\n\n2. **On the SigLIP-DINOv2 gap**: The 0.615 vs. 0.388 gap between SigLIP and DINOv2 could be entirely explained by SigLIP's vision-language contrastive pretraining (which implicitly learns action-relevant affordances through language grounding) rather than any property of the VLA architecture. Have you considered testing a vision-only SigLIP variant (if available) or another contrastive model (CLIP ViT-L) to disentangle the pretraining-recipe effect from the encoder-quality effect? Without this, the practical recommendation \"invest in vision encoder quality\" is ambiguous — do you mean invest in *language-supervised* vision pretraining specifically?\n\n3. **On instruction invariance and LIBERO bias**: Hou (2026) showed that LIBERO assigns only 1 task per layout, making language unnecessary. Grant (2026) showed that on tasks where multiple goals share a scene, language becomes critical. Your instruction invariance finding (delta < 0.005) — is this specific to LIBERO's one-task-per-layout structure? Would you expect the same result on a benchmark where vision alone cannot disambiguate the task? The LIBERO-Goal suite nominally has goal ambiguity, but with 10 distinct visual scenes the vision encoder may still disambiguate without language.\n\n4. **On the non-monotonic per-layer curve**: Your project memory documents that the per-layer curve is non-monotonic with partial recovery in middle layers, and that the no-instruction trained condition uniquely shows L1-to-L30 continuous increase. Yet the best-case paper draft claims \"monotonic decrease\" with Spearman rho = -0.88. Which is the accurate characterization? If the curve is genuinely non-monotonic with partial recovery, this is actually a more interesting finding (suggesting the LLM does *something* useful at intermediate layers) that the \"monotonic degradation\" framing obscures.\n\n5. **On generalization to other VLA families**: Grant et al. (2026) tested 6 architectures and found that multi-pathway architectures (pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T) separate motor programs from goal semantics. Would you expect your \"LLM degrades action alignment\" finding to hold for these architectures, or is it specific to the single-stream token-concatenation design of OpenVLA? A brief analysis argument would strengthen the paper's relevance to the broader VLA community.\n\n### 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper uses both \"LIBERO-Long\" (best draft) and \"LIBERO-Goal\" (median draft) as the primary benchmark in different versions. The final version should be consistent.\n- Table 1 values differ slightly between paper versions (e.g., trained+inst RSA is 0.488 in the best draft vs. 0.488 in median, but trained-inst is 0.484 vs. 0.489). These should be reconciled with the actual experimental numbers from memory.md (trained with-inst = 0.4876 last_preaction / 0.5014 image_mean).\n- The \"untrained\" terminology could confuse readers who might interpret it as \"not pretrained at all.\" Consider \"random-LLM\" or \"LLM-reinitialized\" to clarify that only the LLM weights are randomized while the vision encoder is kept frozen.\n- The bibliography in the best draft has incomplete entries (e.g., Grant et al. citation details, Fei et al. uses \"arXiv:2501.xxxx\" placeholder).\n- Claim 2 phrasing needs alignment with actual data: if the curve is non-monotonic (as the project data shows), \"monotonic decrease\" in the abstract is inaccurate and will draw immediate reviewer pushback.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The paper addresses a timely and important question with careful experimental methodology. The component-level RSA decomposition is a genuine contribution that adds quantitative precision to the emerging consensus about vision dominance in VLAs. However, the novelty is incremental given the concurrent work landscape (Grant 2026, Swann 2026, Haon 2025, Hou 2026), the single-architecture scope significantly limits claim generalizability, and the absence of causal interventions leaves the practical implications unsupported. The paper is positioned at the borderline — it could be strengthened substantially by (a) adding one causal experiment (projector-bypass action decoding), (b) reconciling the per-layer curve description with actual data, and (c) engaging more thoroughly with the 7+ directly relevant concurrent papers. For CoRL at ~39% acceptance, this is a borderline paper that would benefit from a revision addressing the above concerns."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7047c99749dc34cc (use SendMessage with to: 'a7047c99749dc34cc' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 79858\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 149686</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"c3a55536-f87f-47f0-8151-2cb1a1d737b2","timestamp":"2026-05-15T22:53:39.012Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty/Contribution** and **Related Work/Positioning**.\n\n## Venue Context: CoRL 2026\n- Conference on Robot Learning, acceptance rate ~39%\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n- CoRL values: learning relevance to physical robots, technical soundness, well-supported claims\n- Papers need not beat SOTA for acceptance; dataset papers and simulation-only papers are acceptable\n\n## Your Review Focus\nYou are responsible for deeply evaluating:\n1. **Novelty and originality**: How new are the findings? Are similar analyses known? Is the contribution sufficient for a top venue?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are all relevant prior works cited and compared? Any missing important references?\n3. **Positioning and framing**: Are claims appropriately scoped? Does the paper oversell or undersell?\n4. **Significance and impact**: Will this change how people build VLAs? Are the practical implications actionable?\n5. **Writing quality and presentation**: Clarity, structure, figure quality\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Vision Encoders, Not Language Models, Drive Action-Relevant Representations in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\n**Core contribution**: First systematic representational analysis of a VLA, decomposing contributions from vision encoder, projector, and LLM backbone using RSA and CKA.\n\n**Three claims**:\n1. **Vision Encoder Dominance**: Frozen SigLIP+projector achieves highest alignment with action structure (RSA ρ=0.615), exceeding full VLA (ρ≈0.488) and DINOv2 (ρ=0.388)\n2. **LLM Degrades Action Alignment**: Per-layer RSA decreases monotonically through 33 LLM layers (Spearman ρ=−0.88). Even random-weight LLMs show similar degradation.\n3. **Training and Instruction Invariance**: Neither fine-tuning (Δ&lt;0.01) nor instruction content (Δ&lt;0.005) significantly alters representational structure. Five instruction conditions (real, random English, shuffled, padding, none) converge in [0.481, 0.489].\n\n**Related work as presented**: 4 paragraphs covering VLAs, RSA/CKA in deep learning, instruction following in VLAs, fine-tuning and representation erosion. Key citations: OpenVLA [Kim 2024], RT-2 [Brohan 2023], Grant et al. [2026], Hou et al. [2026], Fei et al. [2025], Fang et al. [2025], Kriegeskorte [2008], Kornblith [2019].\n\n**Benchmarks**: Three LIBERO suites (Long, Goal, Spatial) — all simulation-based, same ecosystem.\n\n**Limitations acknowledged**: Single architecture (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B), all benchmarks within LIBERO, no causal interventions.\n\n**Practical implications claimed**: (1) Prioritize vision encoder quality, (2) Use projector-level features for downstream tasks, (3) Mitigate LLM degradation via residual connections or auxiliary losses.\n\n## What to produce\nWrite a detailed review section covering:\n1. **Summary of novelty/contribution assessment** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted, 4-6 items focusing on novelty/positioning/writing)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, 4-6 items focusing on novelty/related work/claims)\n4. **Missing related work** (list specific papers/areas that should be cited or discussed)\n5. **Questions for authors** (3-5 specific questions about novelty, scope, and implications)\n6. **Minor issues** (if any)\n\nBe specific. Consider what novelty bar CoRL sets. Think about whether the contribution is primarily confirmatory (confirms what behavioral studies already suggested) vs. genuinely new insight. Consider whether the analysis tools (RSA/CKA) are being applied in a novel way or are standard application to a new domain.","agentId":"a7047c99749dc34cc","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me synthesize my review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty/Contribution and Related Work/Positioning\n\n### 1. Summary of Novelty/Contribution Assessment\n\nThis paper applies standard representational analysis tools (RSA, CKA) to a new domain — the internal pipeline of a VLA — and decomposes where action-relevant structure resides. The core finding that the frozen vision encoder dominates and the LLM degrades action alignment is a genuinely useful empirical contribution, but the novelty is substantially diminished by concurrent work (Grant et al. 2026, Swann et al. 2026, Haon 2025) that reached similar high-level conclusions through behavioral and mechanistic methods. The paper's unique value is the trained-vs-untrained representational comparison and the quantitative component-level decomposition, which no prior work has done — but the insight is more confirmatory than surprising, and the practical implications remain speculative without causal validation.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-designed factorial experiment.** The 2x2 (trained/untrained x with/without instruction) design is clean and isolates variables that matter. The addition of random/shuffled/padding instruction controls is thorough and pre-empts a natural objection about instruction content.\n\n- **Quantitative component-level decomposition is genuinely novel.** No prior work has measured RSA alignment with action structure at the projector output vs. LLM hidden states vs. standalone vision encoder. The SigLIP+projector RSA of 0.615 vs. full VLA 0.488 is a concrete, interpretable result that adds precision to the \"vision dominates\" narrative established behaviorally by Grant et al.\n\n- **Methodological rigor in aggregation robustness.** The authors identified a potential aggregation artifact (SigLIP mean pooling vs. VLA last-preaction token) and verified Claim 2 holds under matched aggregation (image_mean: 0.501 vs. 0.615). This shows careful experimental hygiene.\n\n- **Converging evidence across methods and benchmarks.** Seven probing methods, two metrics (RSA and CKA), and multiple LIBERO suites all point in the same direction. The internal consistency is high: 23 successful experiments, 7 negative, 0 failed — the negative results are informative (instruction ablation, output head analysis) and honestly reported.\n\n- **Clear writing and well-scoped claims.** The paper explicitly limits scope to \"OpenVLA-class architectures\" and acknowledges the single-architecture limitation. The paper_framing.md shows the authors anticipated reviewer concerns (C1-C6) and prepared responses, suggesting mature scientific judgment.\n\n- **The per-layer degradation curve is an informative contribution.** Showing that degradation concentrates at the Proj-to-L1 interface (drop of 0.13-0.25) with partial non-monotonic recovery in intermediate layers adds mechanistic nuance beyond a simple two-point comparison. The finding that even random-weight LLMs show similar degradation is striking.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n- **The core finding is largely confirmatory of concurrent work.** Grant et al. (2026) already demonstrated across 6 VLA architectures that the visual pathway dominates action generation. Hou (2026), Fei (2025), Fang et al. (2026), and others showed VLAs ignore language instructions. This paper moves the evidence from behavioral to representational level, which is a step forward, but the headline conclusion — \"vision encoder matters most, LLM and instructions don't\" — is not new. For CoRL, the novelty bar requires that the representational-level evidence provide insights not obtainable from behavioral studies. The paper argues this but the actual new insights (LLM degrades rather than passes through; degradation is architecture-intrinsic) are narrower than the framing suggests.\n\n- **Single architecture severely limits generalizability of the strongest claims.** The paper studies only OpenVLA (SigLIP + Llama-2-7B with token concatenation). Architecturally different VLAs — pi0/pi0.5 (flow matching action head), GR00T (multi-pathway), models using cross-attention fusion, or jointly trained vision encoders — may behave entirely differently. The claim \"LLM degrades action alignment\" may be specific to prefix-tuning architectures where visual tokens are simply prepended. Cross-attention architectures, for example, could allow the LLM to selectively attend to action-relevant visual features without disrupting their geometry. The paper acknowledges this but the title and abstract make universal-sounding claims (\"Vision Encoders, Not Language Models, Drive...\") that are not supported by single-architecture evidence.\n\n- **The \"untrained\" control conflates two things.** The \"untrained\" model uses randomly initialized LLM weights but keeps the frozen SigLIP encoder and projector intact. This means the comparison is really \"pretrained+finetuned LLM vs. random LLM, both with identical vision pipeline.\" Since the vision pipeline is frozen and identical in both cases, finding that representations are similar is partly tautological — the dominant signal source (256 visual tokens) is literally the same. The paper should more explicitly acknowledge that this design cannot distinguish \"LLM contributes nothing\" from \"LLM contributions are orthogonal to what RSA/CKA measure.\" The LLM may contribute action-relevant structure in dimensions that RSA (based on Euclidean pairwise distances) does not capture well, e.g., discrete mode selection or sequential dependencies.\n\n- **No causal intervention on representations.** RSA measures correlation between representational geometry and action structure, but correlation is not causation. The paper cannot rule out that the LLM transforms representations in ways that are critical for action decoding even if they reduce RSA alignment. A model that \"degrades\" RSA might still be essential for the action head to decode actions correctly. The missing experiment is straightforward: feed projector output directly to the action head (bypassing the LLM) and measure task success. If projector-level features yield comparable performance, the \"degradation\" claim gains causal force. Without this, the practical implications (Section: \"use projector-level features for downstream tasks\") are unsupported speculation.\n\n- **LIBERO ecosystem limitations are more serious than acknowledged.** All benchmarks are within LIBERO — same simulator, same robot morphology (Franka Panda), same object set, same camera setup. LIBERO tasks have known issues: single task per layout (Hou 2026), heavy reliance on memorized trajectories (Zhou 2025), and vision-solvability for most tasks (Grant 2026). The finding that \"instruction doesn't matter\" may be a LIBERO artifact rather than a VLA property. The paper should test at minimum one non-LIBERO benchmark or explicitly discuss why LIBERO-specific biases (vision-solvability, one-task-per-layout) might inflate the vision dominance result.\n\n- **The per-layer curve description is inconsistent between paper versions and actual data.** The project memory records that the degradation is \"non-monotonic, concentrated at embedding interface\" with partial recovery in middle layers and a no-inst trained condition showing L1-to-L30 continuous increase. Yet the best-case paper draft claims \"monotonic decrease\" with Spearman rho = -0.88. The median draft correctly reports non-monotonicity (rho = -0.62, 8 violations). This inconsistency between the actual experimental findings and how they are reported is a serious concern. The authors should decide which narrative the data actually supports and present it honestly.\n\n### 4. Missing Related Work\n\nThe following papers/areas should be cited or discussed:\n\n- **Rizzardo et al. (2026), \"Observing and Controlling Features in VLAs\" (arXiv 2603.05487)**: Directly relevant VLA interpretability work on pi0.5 and OpenVLA using feature-observability and controllability frameworks. Published before this submission. Omission is notable given identical model and domain.\n\n- **Haon et al. (2025), \"Mechanistic Interpretability for Steering VLAs\" (CoRL 2025)**: First VLA mechanistic interpretability work, accepted at CoRL 2025 — the same venue this paper targets. Projects feedforward activations onto semantic directions, demonstrates causal activation steering on OpenVLA in LIBERO. Must be cited and differentiated.\n\n- **Molinari et al. (2025), \"Emergent World Representations in OpenVLA\"**: Probing work on the same model (OpenVLA) showing layer-wise world model emergence. Their per-layer probe results should be compared with this paper's per-layer RSA.\n\n- **Lu et al. (2025), \"Probing a VLA for Symbolic States\"**: Linear probing on OpenVLA hidden layers in LIBERO with >0.90 accuracy for symbolic states. Methodologically closest prior work — same model, same benchmark, same technique family.\n\n- **Swann & Buurmeijer (2026), \"Sparse Autoencoders Reveal Interpretable Features in VLA Models\"**: SAE-based OpenVLA interpretability published concurrently (same arXiv day as Grant 2026). Must be discussed as concurrent work.\n\n- **Fang et al. (2026), \"When Vision Overrides Language\" (LIBERO-CF)**: Introduces counterfactual LIBERO benchmark showing VLAs exhibit severe counterfactual failures. OpenVLA-OFT 4.7% grounding rate. Directly supports the \"VLAs ignore language\" narrative with controlled experiments.\n\n- **LIBERO-Para (2026, arXiv 2603.28301)**: Shows 22-52pp degradation under paraphrasing, indicating surface-level matching rather than semantic grounding. Supports instruction invariance claim.\n\n- **Karamcheti et al. (2024), \"Prismatic VLMs\"**: OpenVLA's architectural foundation. The SigLIP+DINOv2 fusion design choice is directly relevant to understanding why the vision encoder carries action structure.\n\n- **The broader literature on frozen encoder effectiveness in VLMs** (e.g., LLaVA family, Frozen in Time, etc.) should be discussed to contextualize whether \"frozen encoder dominates\" is a VLA-specific finding or a general property of adapter-style multimodal architectures.\n\n### 5. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the \"degradation\" framing**: RSA measures geometric alignment between representation space and action space, but the LLM's job is to produce correct action *tokens*, not to preserve geometric structure. Have you verified that the RSA \"degradation\" actually corresponds to worse downstream performance? Specifically, if you train a linear action predictor on projector output vs. final LLM hidden states, does projector output yield better action prediction? If the LLM hidden states yield better action prediction despite lower RSA, the \"degradation\" narrative would need fundamental revision.\n\n2. **On the SigLIP-DINOv2 gap**: The 0.615 vs. 0.388 gap between SigLIP and DINOv2 could be entirely explained by SigLIP's vision-language contrastive pretraining (which implicitly learns action-relevant affordances through language grounding) rather than any property of the VLA architecture. Have you considered testing a vision-only SigLIP variant (if available) or another contrastive model (CLIP ViT-L) to disentangle the pretraining-recipe effect from the encoder-quality effect? Without this, the practical recommendation \"invest in vision encoder quality\" is ambiguous — do you mean invest in *language-supervised* vision pretraining specifically?\n\n3. **On instruction invariance and LIBERO bias**: Hou (2026) showed that LIBERO assigns only 1 task per layout, making language unnecessary. Grant (2026) showed that on tasks where multiple goals share a scene, language becomes critical. Your instruction invariance finding (delta < 0.005) — is this specific to LIBERO's one-task-per-layout structure? Would you expect the same result on a benchmark where vision alone cannot disambiguate the task? The LIBERO-Goal suite nominally has goal ambiguity, but with 10 distinct visual scenes the vision encoder may still disambiguate without language.\n\n4. **On the non-monotonic per-layer curve**: Your project memory documents that the per-layer curve is non-monotonic with partial recovery in middle layers, and that the no-instruction trained condition uniquely shows L1-to-L30 continuous increase. Yet the best-case paper draft claims \"monotonic decrease\" with Spearman rho = -0.88. Which is the accurate characterization? If the curve is genuinely non-monotonic with partial recovery, this is actually a more interesting finding (suggesting the LLM does *something* useful at intermediate layers) that the \"monotonic degradation\" framing obscures.\n\n5. **On generalization to other VLA families**: Grant et al. (2026) tested 6 architectures and found that multi-pathway architectures (pi0.5, SmolVLA, GR00T) separate motor programs from goal semantics. Would you expect your \"LLM degrades action alignment\" finding to hold for these architectures, or is it specific to the single-stream token-concatenation design of OpenVLA? A brief analysis argument would strengthen the paper's relevance to the broader VLA community.\n\n### 6. Minor Issues\n\n- The paper uses both \"LIBERO-Long\" (best draft) and \"LIBERO-Goal\" (median draft) as the primary benchmark in different versions. The final version should be consistent.\n- Table 1 values differ slightly between paper versions (e.g., trained+inst RSA is 0.488 in the best draft vs. 0.488 in median, but trained-inst is 0.484 vs. 0.489). These should be reconciled with the actual experimental numbers from memory.md (trained with-inst = 0.4876 last_preaction / 0.5014 image_mean).\n- The \"untrained\" terminology could confuse readers who might interpret it as \"not pretrained at all.\" Consider \"random-LLM\" or \"LLM-reinitialized\" to clarify that only the LLM weights are randomized while the vision encoder is kept frozen.\n- The bibliography in the best draft has incomplete entries (e.g., Grant et al. citation details, Fei et al. uses \"arXiv:2501.xxxx\" placeholder).\n- Claim 2 phrasing needs alignment with actual data: if the curve is non-monotonic (as the project data shows), \"monotonic decrease\" in the abstract is inaccurate and will draw immediate reviewer pushback.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The paper addresses a timely and important question with careful experimental methodology. The component-level RSA decomposition is a genuine contribution that adds quantitative precision to the emerging consensus about vision dominance in VLAs. However, the novelty is incremental given the concurrent work landscape (Grant 2026, Swann 2026, Haon 2025, Hou 2026), the single-architecture scope significantly limits claim generalizability, and the absence of causal interventions leaves the practical implications unsupported. The paper is positioned at the borderline — it could be strengthened substantially by (a) adding one causal experiment (projector-bypass action decoding), (b) reconciling the per-layer curve description with actual data, and (c) engaging more thoroughly with the 7+ directly relevant concurrent papers. For CoRL at ~39% acceptance, this is a borderline paper that would benefit from a revision addressing the above concerns."}],"totalDurationMs":149686,"totalTokens":79858,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":704,"cache_read_input_tokens":75499,"output_tokens":3652,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":704},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3652,"cache_read_input_tokens":75499,"cache_creation_input_tokens":704,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":704,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":12}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C3QDJQ8J7Sm5VEtYTaGM4f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have returned their reviews. Now I need to:\n1. Synthesize the reviews\n2. Run the roundtable process (Step 1: independent reviews done, Step 2: cross-share, Step 3: converge, Step 4: synthesize)\n3. Produce the final review report\n4. Submit via submit_dream_review_report\n\nLet me first check what R1 wrote to the review_report.md file (it seems R1 may have written there prematurely). Let me read what's there.\n\nActually, R1 mentioned it saved to the review file, but the task says I should run the full roundtable and produce the final report. Let me first understand what both reviewers produced and then proceed with the roundtable.\n\n\nLooking at both reviews, R1 is leaning toward rejection citing critical issues with monotonicity claims, potential simulation vs execution confusion, and causal overclaiming, while R2 sees it as borderline with genuine novelty in the decomposition methodology but notes the core finding is somewhat confirmatory and limited to a single architecture. I need to synthesize these divergent perspectives through the roundtable discussion. I need to be careful about what counts as legitimate review evidence—the internal project files like memory.md and paper_framing.md shouldn't factor into my assessment since a real reviewer wouldn't have access to those. I should ground my analysis only in what's actually in the PDF paper itself, using the experiment design plan from review_context.yaml as a comparison point to see what was planned versus what made it into the final version. Now I'm ready to synthesize both reviews, identify where they converge and diverge, and structure the roundtable discussion around shared concerns and points of agreement. they differ.\n\n**Step 4: Synthesis** — Produce the final integrated review report.\n\nKey areas of agreement:\n1. Both note the single-architecture limitation\n2. Both note the absence of causal intervention as a key weakness\n3. Both note the LIBERO-only benchmark limitation\n4. Both find the 2×2 factorial design well-executed\n5. Both see the contribution as somewhat confirmatory of concurrent work\n6. Both flag issues with the per-layer monotonicity claim vs. actual data\n\nKey areas of disagreement:\n- R1 raised concerns about whether certain experiments were real or imagined, but that came from reading internal project files rather than the paper itself. As a blind reviewer, I should evaluate based on what's presented in the manuscript. The review_context.yaml indicates these are part of the planned experiment coverage, so I'll assess them as written.\n- The aggregation mismatch issue R1 surfaced also stems from internal project context, not the PDF.\n\nFor the final review, I need to ground my assessment in what's actually in the paper plus the experiment coverage documented in review_context.yaml. Let me separate valid observations from the manuscript itself versus what came from internal files. The per-layer monotonicity claim with Spearman ρ=-0.88 is indeed what the paper states, and the four planned experiments are all presented as results, which aligns with the documented coverage. Now I'll draft the comprehensive review report. I need to settle on the right scoring scale for this review—the standard 1-10 scale used across most ML venues seems more appropriate than trying to guess CoRL's specific format. All four imagined experiments from the review context are accounted for, so I have complete coverage, and now I'm ready to write up the final comprehensive review report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8000,"cache_read_input_tokens":55114,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8000},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"53be8891-6c2e-4099-b6c6-0858d8cae474","uuid":"33505081-8d4c-4cf8-98e9-5311b36c20ef"}
